title: The Importance of the King James Bible – A Timeless Legacy
description: How God preserved His Word for the English-speaking world: the history of the King James Version, its precision (including the thee, thou, ye, you pronouns), fun Bible facts and how the Bible is arranged.
output: lessons/doctrine/doctrine-week-4-importance-of-the-kjv.html

# Doctrine Week 4. One deck from two old pages: the main page ("The Importance of the KJV") and the separate
# "Pronouns in the KJV Bible: Singular vs. Plural" chart, which is now the table slide in section IV.

=== Introduction
--- The Importance of the King James Bible [title]
overline: Faith Baptist Church of Plymouth County · Adult Sunday School · Week 4
heading: The Importance of the King James Bible
sub: A Timeless Legacy: how God preserved His Word for the English-speaking world
p: In 1604, King James I of England commissioned renowned scholars to create a unified English Bible, birthing the KJV in 1611. This masterpiece shaped Christianity worldwide, becoming the spiritual cornerstone for the Pilgrims, fueling Baptist growth in America, and empowering missionaries to spread the Gospel across every continent.
note: 1604–1611
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

=== I. A Timeless Legacy
--- A Timeless Legacy (1 of 2)
lead: Fun facts about the King James, or Authorized, Version.
panel: 47 Scholars, One Epic Team | **1604 Commission.** 54 revisers were initially approved, but 47 brilliant minds crafted the KJV.
panel: Six Predecessors Paved the Way | **Earlier English Bibles.** Built on Wycliffe’s (1384), Tyndale’s (1525), Coverdale’s (1535), Matthew’s (1537), Great (1539), and Bishops’ (1568) Bibles, the KJV refined them all.
panel: Seven Years of Sweat | **1604 to 1611.** Scholars toiled to translate from Hebrew, Greek, and Aramaic, ensuring accuracy and poetic flair.
panel: Six Companies, Divide and Conquer | **Westminster · Oxford · Cambridge.** Split into six groups, two each in Westminster, Oxford, and Cambridge, they tackled different Bible sections for precision.

--- A Timeless Legacy (2 of 2)
panel: Tyndale’s Ghost Lives On | **84% NT · 76% OT.** About 84% of the KJV’s New Testament and 76% of its Old Testament echo William Tyndale’s groundbreaking work.
panel: The “He Bible,” 1611 | **First edition.** The first 1611 edition, dubbed the “He Bible,” is estimated at 1,500 to 2,000 copies; the exact number is not known.
panel: No Calvinist Notes Allowed | **Royal Approval.** King James banned the Geneva Bible’s Puritan notes, ensuring the KJV was a neutral, royal-approved text.

=== Martyred for the Word
--- William Tyndale: A Bible Translator Executed for Heresy
kicker: Martyred for the Word
lead: William Tyndale was a prominent figure during the Protestant Reformation who was burned at the stake in 1536, executed for “heresy” by the Roman Catholic Church for translating the Bible into English and promoting its accessibility to the common people.

--- Why Was Translating the Bible Considered “Heretical”?
kicker: William Tyndale
p: **Challenging Church Authority:** The Catholic Church held absolute authority on religious issues and guarded its power. Translations of the Bible into vernacular languages like English threatened that authority.
p: Providing the Bible in English allowed literate individuals to read and interpret Scripture for themselves, often arriving at conclusions that differed from unbiblical Catholic doctrine.
quote: “Lord, open the King of England’s eyes.” | William Tyndale, before his execution
p: This prayer was partially answered when King Henry VIII later allowed the English Bible to be available in every parish church.

=== II. Translation, Text & Impact
--- Translation, Text & Impact
lead: The King James Version was created by a team of 47 scholars (54 were originally appointed), including the greatest linguists of the time, from various backgrounds, ensuring a more comprehensive and accurate translation.
panel: Collaboration | **Textual Sources.** Translators of the KJV utilized earlier translations and the original preserved manuscripts: the Masoretic Text for the Old Testament, and the Antioch manuscripts, called “The Received Text,” or Textus Receptus, for the New Testament, striving for accuracy while adapting the language to be accessible and beautiful to the English-speaking public.
panel: Literary Style | **A Literary Masterpiece.** The KJV is renowned for its poetic and rhythmic style, making it not only a religious text but also a literary masterpiece that has influenced English literature significantly.
panel: Cultural Impact | **400 Years of Influence.** The King James Version has left an indelible mark on Christianity, used for 400 years to win souls on every continent and country, shaping the culture, language, and literature of the world, and the expressions and idioms that remain in common use even today.

--- Its Powerful, Preserved Nature
callout: The King James Version has profoundly influenced literature, language, and art, but its powerful, divinely inspired and preserved nature, as the complete Word of God without error in English, the “lingua franca” of this age, has shaped nations, influenced kings and presidents, converted millions, fed the spirit of believers, and served as the rock of Christianity since 1611.

=== III. Lesser-Known KJV Facts
--- Lesser-Known KJV Facts (1 of 2)
panel-warn: Oxford & Cambridge Tweak It | **1762 & 1769 Revisions.** Revised editions in 1769 (Oxford) and 1762 (Cambridge) standardized spelling, punctuation, and italics for clarity.
panel-warn: Apocrypha? Not Inspired! | **1611 Edition.** The 1611 KJV included the Apocrypha but clearly marked it as non-inspired, separating it from canonical Scripture.
panel-warn: Sinners’ Bible Blunder | **1631 Misprint.** A 1631 misprint, dubbed the “Sinners’ Bible,” omitted “not” from [[Exodus 20:14]], reading “Thou shalt commit adultery”!

--- Lesser-Known KJV Facts (2 of 2)
panel-warn: Printer’s Pricey Mistake | **Robert Barker, 1631.** The “Sinners’ Bible” led to a £300 fine for printer Robert Barker, big money in 1631!
panel-warn: Best-Selling Book Ever | **Global Reach.** The KJV holds the title as the world’s top-selling book, with billions of copies distributed globally.
panel-warn: No Copyright (Outside England) | **Crown Copyright.** Free to print worldwide, except in England, where the Crown holds rights, fueling its global spread.

=== IV. Precision in Translation
--- “Are Saved”: the Greek Behind Eternal Security
lead: Why the exact words matter: the KJV’s careful rendering guards doctrine that looser translations can blur.
p: The Greek *σῳζομένοις* (sōzomenois) is a present passive participle meaning “to those saved.” The KJV renders it **“are saved”**, a settled, present reality accomplished by God. Modern versions often render it “are being saved,” an ongoing-process wording that can imply salvation is incomplete or losable.

--- The Power of God to Them That Are Saved
fig: The Power of God to Them That Are Saved | 1 Corinthians 1:18 | **Meaning:** The word of the cross is the power of God precisely to those who stand in the state of having been saved. God’s effective, completed work secures the believer.
fig: If Ye Keep in Memory | 1 Corinthians 15:2 | **Meaning:** The “if” clause is not a condition for retaining salvation but the evidence of genuine faith. True believers hold fast because they are already saved.

--- Supporting the Eternally Secure Reading
refs: Philippians 1:6; John 10:28-29; Romans 8:30; Ephesians 2:8-9

--- Thee, Thou, Ye, You: the KJV’s Built-In Precision
lead: Modern English collapsed singular and plural “you” into one word. The KJV never did. Its pronouns tell you at a glance whether God (or a speaker) is addressing one person or a whole group.
p: The next two slides chart the pronouns of the KJV, singular and plural, by type and number.

--- Pronouns in the KJV Bible: Singular
table: Pronoun | Type | Number
tr: I | Personal (subject) | Singular
tr: Me | Personal (object) | Singular
tr: My / Mine | Possessive | Singular
tr: **Thou** | Personal (subject) | Singular
tr: **Thee** | Personal (object) | Singular
tr: **Thy / Thine** | Possessive | Singular
tr: He | Personal (subject) | Singular
tr: Him | Personal (object) | Singular
tr: His | Possessive | Singular
tr: She | Personal (subject) | Singular
tr: Her / Hers | Personal (object) / Possessive | Singular
tr: It | Personal | Singular
tr: Its | Possessive | Singular

--- Pronouns in the KJV Bible: Plural
table: Pronoun | Type | Number
tr: We | Personal (subject) | Plural
tr: Us | Personal (object) | Plural
tr: Our / Ours | Possessive | Plural
tr: **Ye** | Personal (subject) | Plural
tr: **You** | Personal (object) | Plural
tr: **Your / Yours** | Possessive | Plural
tr: They | Personal (subject) | Plural
tr: Them | Personal (object) | Plural
tr: Their / Theirs | Possessive | Plural

=== V. Fun Bible Facts
--- Fun Bible Facts (1 of 4)
panel: Epic Timespan | **4,284 years covered.** From creation in Genesis to John’s writing of Revelation (c. AD 95–97), the Bible covers approximately 4,284 years. The writing itself spans about 1,500 years, from Moses (c. 1400 BC) penning the first five books to Revelation (c. AD 95).
panel: 40+ Authors, One God | **Kings, shepherds, prophets, fishermen.** Written by over 40 diverse authors, inspired by the Holy Spirit.
panel: Prophecy Power | **1,800+ prophecies.** Over 1,800 prophecies, with 300+ about Jesus fulfilled perfectly, like His Bethlehem birth ([[Micah 5:2]]).

--- Fun Bible Facts (2 of 4)
panel: Self-Referencing Scripture | **“It is written.”** The Bible cites itself over 1,000 times, affirming its own authority.
panel: The KJV Is Its Own Dictionary | **First appearance rule.** You can generally find the meaning of a word by looking for its first appearance and seeing the context.
panel: Year-Long Read | **1,189 chapters.** Read 3.3 chapters daily to finish in a year, or read 30 minutes daily and finish in under 5 months!

--- Fun Bible Facts (3 of 4)
panel: 66 Books, One Story | **39 OT + 27 NT.** Thirty-nine Old Testament and twenty-seven New Testament books weave God’s redemptive plan from Genesis to Revelation.
panel: Verses Galore | **31,102 verses.** Packed with wisdom, history, and God’s Word for all.
panel: No Contradictions | **Divine inspiration.** Despite centuries and 40+ authors, the Bible’s message stays unified, a testament to divine inspiration.

--- Fun Bible Facts (4 of 4)
panel: Three Tongues | **Hebrew · Aramaic · Greek.** Written in Hebrew (Old Testament), Aramaic (parts of Daniel and Ezra), and Greek (New Testament).
panel: Shortest & Longest Chapters | **[[Psalm 117:1-2|Psalm 117]] & [[Psalm 119:1-176|Psalm 119]].** Psalm 117 (2 verses) is the shortest; Psalm 119 (176 verses) is the longest, a meditation on God’s Word.
panel: Shortest Verse | **[[John 11:35]].** “Jesus wept,” shows Christ’s humanity and mercy in just two words.

=== VI. How the KJV Bible Is Broken Down
--- How the KJV Bible Is Broken Down: Old Testament
lead: 66 books, grouped by purpose and style, telling God’s story from creation to eternity.
table: Category, number of books and theme | The books
tr: **Pentateuch**: 5 books · God’s Creation, the Law, Israel’s Origins | Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers, Deuteronomy.
tr: **Historical Books**: 12 books · Conquest to Exile to Return | Joshua, Judges, Ruth, 1 & 2 Samuel, 1 & 2 Kings, 1 & 2 Chronicles, Ezra, Nehemiah, Esther.
tr: **Poetic / Wisdom Books**: 5 books · Songs, Prayers, Wisdom | Job, Psalms, Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, Song of Solomon.
tr: **Major Prophets**: 5 books · God’s Word Through Bold Prophets | Isaiah, Jeremiah/Lamentations, Ezekiel, Daniel.
tr: **Minor Prophets**: 12 books · Short, Powerful Prophetic Books | Hosea, Joel, Amos, Obadiah, Jonah, Micah, Nahum, Habakkuk, Zephaniah, Haggai, Zechariah, Malachi.

--- How the KJV Bible Is Broken Down: New Testament
table: Category, number of books and theme | The books
tr: **Gospels**: 4 books · Jesus’ Life, Death, and Resurrection | Matthew, Mark, Luke, John.
tr: **History**: 1 book · The Early Church’s Growth | Acts.
tr: **Pauline Epistles**: 14 books · Paul’s Letters to Churches and Leaders | Romans, 1 & 2 Corinthians, Galatians, Ephesians, Philippians, Colossians, 1 & 2 Thessalonians, 1 & 2 Timothy, Titus, Philemon, Hebrews.
tr: **General Epistles**: 7 books · Letters for All Believers | James, 1 & 2 Peter, 1, 2 & 3 John, Jude.
tr: **Prophecy**: 1 book · Christ’s Return and Final Victory | Revelation.

=== Purified Seven Times
